00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:18.778
This year its going to be
a toll road investment.

00:04:18.778 --> 00:04:21.920
So on to what project finance is.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.628
So project finance involves the ring
fenced financing of an infrastructure

00:04:26.628 --> 00:04:30.232
asset based on the microeconomics
of the project itself,

00:04:30.232 --> 00:04:35.619
as opposed to the sponsor balance sheet or
the city, or government's balance sheet.

00:04:35.619 --> 00:04:39.379
So infrastructure projects,
they have a few distinct tasks

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:43.738
that are required to complete them and
manage them over the long term.

00:04:43.738 --> 00:04:47.602
So they have to be designed, built,
financed, and then operated and

00:04:47.602 --> 00:04:48.397
maintained.

00:04:48.397 --> 00:04:51.676
Under traditional
infrastructure procurement,

00:04:51.676 --> 00:04:56.629
the government essentially hires
companies to complete all of those tasks,

00:04:56.629 --> 00:05:01.582
or uses internal staff to complete some
of them but it generally procures them

00:05:01.582 --> 00:05:05.875
separately and manages the interfaces
between those contracts.

00:05:05.875 --> 00:05:07.643
Under a project finance or

00:05:07.643 --> 00:05:12.221
as it's also referred to as
a public-private partnership or P3,

00:05:12.221 --> 00:05:17.285
essentially the government procures
one contract for a private partner to

00:05:17.285 --> 00:05:22.290
complete all of those paths and
then manage the interfaces between them.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.778
The governments usually use
this procurement model,

00:05:25.778 --> 00:05:30.477
the prime driver is to transfer the risk
of infrastructure development to

00:05:30.477 --> 00:05:32.462
that private sector partner.

00:05:32.462 --> 00:05:36.951
So infrastructure projects are some
of the most risky enterprises that

00:05:36.951 --> 00:05:38.608
governments undertake.

00:05:38.608 --> 00:05:43.335
I like to bucket the risks associated
with infrastructure development

00:05:43.335 --> 00:05:44.768
into three buckets.

00:05:44.768 --> 00:05:49.641
So one, it can of course take much
longer to build than expected and

00:05:49.641 --> 00:05:53.658
go way over budget and
those are commonly correlated.

00:05:53.658 --> 00:05:56.166
It can also cost more to operate and

00:05:56.166 --> 00:06:00.926
maintain the asset than the government
originally forecasts and

00:06:00.926 --> 00:06:06.634
then finally, for projects that are funded
by a user fee, such as a toll road,

00:06:06.634 --> 00:06:12.030
there can be lower demand than what
the government originally expected.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.516
So that could cause a lack
of funding in the future.

00:06:16.516 --> 00:06:20.314
So governments as I mentioned generally
use this procurement model to

00:06:20.314 --> 00:06:24.600
transfer risk to the private partner
in developing and maintaining an asset.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:29.068
So there's a few criteria that
we recommend for governments or

00:06:29.068 --> 00:06:33.791
project sponsors to consider when
they're assessing a potential

00:06:33.791 --> 00:06:37.011
project as a potential for
project finance.

00:06:37.011 --> 00:06:40.445
And so the first of those is simply scale.

00:06:40.445 --> 00:06:43.340
Larger projects generally
entail more risk, so

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:48.433
this procurement model is really mostly
suited to larger infrastructure projects.

00:06:48.433 --> 00:06:53.647
The second factor is general project risk,
so has the sponsor done one of these

00:06:53.647 --> 00:06:58.545
projects before, how did that go,
is this a particularly unique project

00:06:58.545 --> 00:07:03.210
where there might be significant
ONM risk or construction cost risk.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.960
The third factor is flexibility.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.920
So because of these, project finance
generally entails a longer contract.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:18.898
Generally if there's a need for
flexibility due to technology change or

00:07:18.898 --> 00:07:22.619
other factors, this procurement
model might not be a good fit for

00:07:22.619 --> 00:07:25.951
that because it's generally used for
longer term contracts.

00:07:25.951 --> 00:07:28.780
And then finally,
the last factor is innovation.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:34.434
So this procurement model because it
combines construction and operation and

00:07:34.434 --> 00:07:38.941
maintenance into one contract,
there's opportunities for innovation for

00:07:38.941 --> 00:07:43.582
the contractor that's building the asset
because they have to both build it and

00:07:43.582 --> 00:07:46.030
operate and maintain it in the long term.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:50.818
So that means they might make decisions
during construction to optimize for

00:07:50.818 --> 00:07:55.325
lifecycle costs, instead of just
the initial cost to build the asset.

00:07:55.325 --> 00:07:59.631
So this slide shows the many parties
involved in a complex infrastructure

00:07:59.631 --> 00:08:02.210
project and
how they relate to one another.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.378
Under a project finance structure.

00:08:06.378 --> 00:08:11.245
So generally a special purpose vehicle or
an SPV is created with the sole purpose

00:08:11.245 --> 00:08:14.887
of building and
maintaining the infrastructure project.

00:08:14.887 --> 00:08:18.695
The SPV will have equity investors,
it will arrange for

00:08:18.695 --> 00:08:23.719
financing to build the project, and
then it will also have contracts with

00:08:23.719 --> 00:08:28.514
the many contractors, and or
operators needed to build the project.

00:08:28.514 --> 00:08:32.329
It'll also have a project development
agreement with the sponsoring government.

00:08:34.251 --> 00:08:38.627
So, onto our topic for today.

00:08:38.627 --> 00:08:40.988
So to get straight to the point,

00:08:40.988 --> 00:08:45.887
this procurement model isn't used
in the United States at the same

00:08:45.887 --> 00:08:50.365
rate as it's used in other
developing economies globally.

00:08:50.365 --> 00:08:55.805
So I like Canada and Australia as good
comparators to the US because they're

00:08:55.805 --> 00:09:00.573
also large land mass developed
economies and federal democracy.

00:09:00.573 --> 00:09:05.760
And this chart shows the usage of this
procurement model in Australia and

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.586
Canada, both in terms of
the capital attracted, and

00:09:09.586 --> 00:09:13.450
the number of projects compared to the US.

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:17.804
So it's pretty clear that it's used
much less in the United States which by

00:09:17.804 --> 00:09:22.174
population of course has a much larger
economy than Canada and Australia.

00:09:22.174 --> 00:09:27.500
The question is why hasn't this model
picked up in the United States.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:32.223
Well I think this chart shows
that it's not due to demand.

00:09:32.223 --> 00:09:35.986
So this chart shows all
of the transportation and

00:09:35.986 --> 00:09:42.505
social public-private partnerships that
had been procured in the United States,

00:09:42.505 --> 00:09:47.280
and it's sourced from
an industry publication.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.512
So the start of each of these bars
indicates the start of the procurement

00:09:51.512 --> 00:09:54.063
process from the sponsoring government.

00:09:54.063 --> 00:09:58.408
So, typically when a government
pursues one of these projects,

00:09:58.408 --> 00:10:02.437
it starts by issuing a request for
qualifications, or RFQ,

00:10:02.437 --> 00:10:06.469
to potential companies that
would compete for the project.

00:10:06.469 --> 00:10:11.811
The end of this bar is commercial close,
or when the government effectively

00:10:11.811 --> 00:10:16.486
signs a contract with that
infrastructure developer to then go and

00:10:16.486 --> 00:10:19.088
finance and then build the project.

00:10:19.088 --> 00:10:25.840
For the red bars, the end of the bar is
commercial close, or the contract signing.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:29.980
For the grey bars,
its when the project was cancelled.

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:33.147
So I think this charts
indicates a couple of trends.

00:10:33.147 --> 00:10:34.204
One is that demand for

00:10:34.204 --> 00:10:37.739
this procurement model in
the United States is certainly increasing.

00:10:37.739 --> 00:10:41.960
You can kind of see that by the start
of procurement is kind of accelerating

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:42.650
over time.

00:10:44.155 --> 00:10:49.278
What's also very clear from
this chart is that successful

00:10:49.278 --> 00:10:54.308
procurements are not being
completed at that same rate.

00:10:54.308 --> 00:10:58.731
So in general, this procurement model does
take a little bit longer to procure in

00:10:58.731 --> 00:11:03.299
the United States when you compare with
other developed economies, like Europe.

00:11:03.299 --> 00:11:10.204
But what makes the US particularly unique
is the prevalence of project cancellation.

00:11:10.204 --> 00:11:13.100
So you can see a lot
of gray on this chart.

00:11:15.479 --> 00:11:20.276
So this chart further illustrates
what I think is an explanation of

00:11:20.276 --> 00:11:22.686
this trend in the United States.

00:11:22.686 --> 00:11:27.463
So this shows a breakdown of
infrastructure spending, both by capital

00:11:27.463 --> 00:11:32.810
projects, or spending for new
infrastructure, and operation maintenance.

00:11:34.255 --> 00:11:37.743
It's also broken down by the federal
government, and then our state and

00:11:37.743 --> 00:11:38.783
local governments.

00:11:38.783 --> 00:11:44.670
So I think this chart debunks a bit
of a myth about US infrastructure.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:49.139
So a lot of media reports have indicated
that part of the problem with US

00:11:49.139 --> 00:11:53.540
infrastructure is essentially
that politicians don't prioritize

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:56.521
infrastructure enough
in the United States.

00:11:56.521 --> 00:12:00.842
And this indicates to me that this
is only partially true, mainly for

00:12:00.842 --> 00:12:02.494
our federal government.

00:12:02.494 --> 00:12:07.491
State and local governments, on the other
hand, are actually increasing spending in

00:12:07.491 --> 00:12:13.580
real dollars to infrastructure and they're
particularly increasing it to maintenance.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.620
So there's been a big shift, essentially,

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:21.100
in responsibility in the United States
from spending primarily by

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:25.742
the federal government to spending
by state and local governments.

00:12:25.742 --> 00:12:28.769
This slide indicates part
of my explanation for

00:12:28.769 --> 00:12:33.385
why this procurement model of using
project finance hasn't picked up in

00:12:33.385 --> 00:12:37.107
the United States as it has
in other developed economies.

00:12:37.107 --> 00:12:43.085
So to explain this rather quickly,
this chart shows infrastructure

00:12:43.085 --> 00:12:49.588
spending over time both on capital
projects and on maintenance projects.

00:12:49.588 --> 00:12:54.235
And so we use an institutional explanation
for the unique circumstances of

00:12:54.235 --> 00:13:00.380
infrastructure development in the United
States as it relates to project finance.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:04.276
So essentially what I mean by that
is a lot of our institutions,

00:13:04.276 --> 00:13:07.730
and by that I mean our public agencies for
developing and

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:11.409
maintaining infrastructure at
the state and local level,

00:13:11.409 --> 00:13:16.143
were formed during very high levels of
spending by the federal government.

00:13:16.143 --> 00:13:19.780
And most of that spending
was focused on capital cost.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:26.069
Now federal spending is still primarily
oriented towards capital costs,

00:13:26.069 --> 00:13:30.855
but the world that we exist in
in the United States is a much

00:13:30.855 --> 00:13:35.745
different place than in
the decades following World War II

00:13:35.745 --> 00:13:40.859
when heavy federal spending
was supporting capital costs.

00:13:40.859 --> 00:13:45.051
And our public institutions
were essentially designed for

00:13:45.051 --> 00:13:50.162
that world as opposed to the situation
that we're currently in with that

00:13:50.162 --> 00:13:55.132
broad shift in responsibility towards
our state and local agencies.

00:13:55.132 --> 00:13:59.530
So part of our explanation is that our
public institutions in the United States,

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:03.603
primarily at the state and local level,
aren't really designed to manage

00:14:03.603 --> 00:14:06.902
procurements using project
finance which has to account for

00:14:06.902 --> 00:14:10.974
all of the life cycle costs of an asset
as opposed to just capital costs which

00:14:10.974 --> 00:14:13.330
are supported by the federal government.

00:14:16.226 --> 00:14:20.157
So this is my last complex chart for
today, so

00:14:20.157 --> 00:14:25.228
this is that same data on
public-private partnerships,

00:14:25.228 --> 00:14:31.029
transportation and social
infrastructure broken down by state.

00:14:31.029 --> 00:14:36.317
So the x axis shows the number of
projects that have been procured.

00:14:36.317 --> 00:14:40.856
The size of each bubble is the relative
amount of capital that the state's been

00:14:40.856 --> 00:14:43.626
able to attract using
this procurement model.

00:14:43.626 --> 00:14:48.051
And then the y axis is
the average procurement time for

00:14:48.051 --> 00:14:50.125
projects in that state.

00:14:50.125 --> 00:14:54.137
Now I use this chart to
illustrate basically the power

00:14:54.137 --> 00:14:56.277
of institutional change for

00:14:56.277 --> 00:15:02.180
public agencies in developing a new
procurement model like project finance.

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:08.187
So every state that has procured more
than one of these projects, essentially

00:15:08.187 --> 00:15:13.841
has undergone some form of institutional
change in its public agencies.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:21.808
Just to highlight a few, so Texas, Florida
and Virginia have all created P3 offices

00:15:21.808 --> 00:15:27.778
within their departments of transportation
with varying levels of autonomy.

00:15:27.778 --> 00:15:32.697
Colorado has created the High Performance
Transportation Enterprise which procures

00:15:32.697 --> 00:15:34.050
projects in its state.

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:38.252
Indiana created
the Indiana Finance Authority which has

00:15:38.252 --> 00:15:40.185
procured all of its P3s.

00:15:40.185 --> 00:15:44.318
And then the city of Long Beach
is a pretty unique example and

00:15:44.318 --> 00:15:49.610
it's actually our case study for
this morning, has procured two of the last

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:54.902
three public-private partnerships in
California just by one city alone,

00:15:54.902 --> 00:16:02.510
and we'll talk a little bit more about
that in a minute So none of these agencies

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:08.560
would be a kind of fully
autonomous alternative

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:13.500
procurement agency like those that have
been formed in Canada and Australia.

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:15.980
So I list a few here,
a couple in Canada and

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:20.790
a couple in In Australia that have
been created to kind of manage this

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:24.090
very different form of infrastructure
procurement within their states.

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:26.070
These are all states or
provincial agencies.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:30.250
So, all of these agencies are different,
but

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:32.810
they share some common characteristics.

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:38.120
One is that they're not sector based,
so they report either to the treasury or

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:42.050
directly into the state executive.

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:44.610
All of them manage

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:49.010
infrastructure procurements that
are completed using project finance or

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:53.410
another kind of alternative structure for
an infrastructure project.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:58.210
These agencies are really designed
to have the internal resources and

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:03.940
the capacity to manage those
alternative procurements as opposed to

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:07.220
traditional infrastructure,
which is very different.

00:17:07.220 --> 00:17:12.270
They also all have an important
role in reporting.

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:16.380
Because project finance
involves a long-term contract,

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:20.940
reporting is a special importance
both on a programmatic level,

00:17:20.940 --> 00:17:24.900
and these agencies issue specific
reports on their projects.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:29.570
And so
to kind of explain the trend of Canada and

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.730
Australia adopting this procurement
model at a faster rate, which we talked

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:37.530
about earlier, all those projects were
procured by organizations like these.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:43.590
So what to do about this
situation in the United States?

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:47.010
Well, we have a few
policy recommendations.

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:50.610
And these are specifically
relating to federal policy.

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:55.570
So federal support for
mega projects is especially important,

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:58.720
especially projects that cross state or
other institutional lines.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:04.580
Lending support programs that are already
in place or that are ramping up for

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:09.330
project finance loans are also
a helpful policy program and

00:18:09.330 --> 00:18:10.950
these are already in place.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:15.500
Enabling project finance fact

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:20.430
projects to access the municipal bond
market is also a good federal policy.

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:24.480
Some of these are in place,
and others have been proposed.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:27.912
More importantly, I think,

00:18:27.912 --> 00:18:34.290
is the federal government's institutional
sport for state and local government.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:39.100
It's especially important so
the Build America Bureau,

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:43.320
which is newly formed, and
the Transportation Investment Center.

00:18:43.320 --> 00:18:49.830
These are both programs that help stage
your local government, transition

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:56.900
or use this procurement model and
they distribute eventually best practices.

00:18:56.900 --> 00:18:58.440
Those are our strong initiatives.

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:07.041
And then perhaps most importantly, one
of my big federal policy recommendations

00:19:07.041 --> 00:19:12.805
is to create programs that fund
projects based on the practices or

00:19:12.805 --> 00:19:19.560
innovative programs as opposed to just the
economic benefits of the project itself.

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:23.050
So the new grant program at
the Department of Transportation,

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:27.610
the INFRA Grant program,
I think is a step in the right direction.

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:33.940
I think that program could be
significantly expanded to even provide

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:38.640
funding support for infrastructure
programs like the creation of

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:43.750
a new infrastructure agency as opposed to
just supporting an innovative project.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:53.680
So now I'll spend a few minutes talking
about our specific case study for today.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:58.120
This is the case of
the Long Beach Civic Center which is

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:00.240
currently under construction.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:04.530
I really like this project because
it illustrates a lot of the concepts

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:08.422
that I've talked about in terms
of the project finance basis.

00:20:08.422 --> 00:20:12.240
It illustrates some
procurement concepts for

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:17.280
how cities can use this procurement
model and it's a good case for

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:21.710
a lot of the topics that we'll
talk about in my course.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:25.840
So this project is
essentially to tear down and

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:30.000
replace the city hall.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.420
It's going to tear it down.

00:20:31.420 --> 00:20:35.300
The project will build a new
city hall to replace it.

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:39.510
The project will also develop
a new headquarters for

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:43.376
the city's port authority,
a new library and a new public park.

00:20:43.376 --> 00:20:48.350
Once that project is developed,

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:53.000
the company that's building it is going
to be responsible for operating and

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:58.340
maintaining the buildings themselves for
a 35- year concession.

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:06.377
The total financing costs for the project
were about just over $500 million.

00:21:08.762 --> 00:21:12.920
So what made this project
a successful procurement?

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:15.320
It's still under construction currently.

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:20.750
Well, the reason the city was
replacing its city hall was

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:25.920
because back in 2005, the city determined
that it was seismically deficient.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:31.420
The building was about 40 years old, and
it was rapidly decaying and the city

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:37.140
determined that they needed to replace
it because it didn't meet seismic code.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:41.360
The city didn't replace it initially,
but they conducted some studies.

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:47.323
And in 2013, the city chose to start
pursuing a public-private partnership.

00:21:47.323 --> 00:21:51.630
And I think there's a few reasons
that the city chose this procurement

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:55.580
model as opposed to traditional
infrastructure procurement.

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:59.210
One is that the first municipal building,

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:04.140
P3, in the United States was also
developed in the city of Long Beach.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:09.420
So as a researcher on
institutional development within

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:15.140
the infrastructure industry,
I think that's an excellent trend, and

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:21.690
that the city that procured the second
project using this procurement model was,

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:28.000
of course, the city that had the first
use of this procurement model.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:33.090
Another factor that led
the city to use this project

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:37.980
finance to develop this
project was that the existing

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:43.200
facility was deteriorating at a much
more rapid pace than the city expected.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:45.630
So there was a real deferred
maintenance problem.

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:50.940
So when the city first came up with
the cost estimate to rehabilitate

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:57.060
the old building in 2006,
they came up with an estimate.

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:59.260
As they were considering options for

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:03.900
the redevelopment of the city hall, they'd
been escalating the cost to rehab the old

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:08.858
building at 5% in their
internal estimates.

00:23:08.858 --> 00:23:10.860
When they reassessed the building in 2013,

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:15.320
their estimate they found
that an additional more

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:19.720
than $30 million of deferred maintenance
had occurred on the building.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:22.510
So the building was deteriorating
at a much more rapid

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:24.690
rate than they originally expected.

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:30.010
The third factor for this project,
and probably the most important

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:35.250
in kind of driving the city to
use this procurement model,

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:41.210
is the city simply calculated their
annual cost to maintain the old facility.

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:43.940
So, they came up with an estimate
that they were spending around 12 and

00:23:43.940 --> 00:23:47.790
half million dollars a year
on the old facility.

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:53.030
And the city essentially just went to the
market and said, we'll spend $12.5 million

00:23:53.030 --> 00:23:59.360
a year and no more on the new facility,
in terms of an annual payment.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:02.900
And the city said it would
index that to inflation.

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:08.110
So how did the city eventually
get a much larger project for

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:09.770
that same annual payment?

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:16.130
So instead of just replacing the city
hall, the project is going to build

00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:20.360
a new port authority building,
a new library and a new city park.

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:25.840
Well, part of the factor there is
that the old facility was sitting

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.320
on a lot of unused public land.

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:32.670
So the developer of this project,
once they complete all of those other

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:38.240
facilities, they're going to be permitted
to develop some residential and

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:41.560
commercial real estate on some
of that unused public land.

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:46.110
So the city effectively used some
of that unused land to offset

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:48.442
the capital cost of
developing a new city hall.

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:51.420
One of the other important factors for

00:24:51.420 --> 00:24:57.250
this project was a performance-based
maintenance requirement.

00:24:57.250 --> 00:25:01.570
So the contractor that builds the new
facilities is going to be responsible for

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:03.680
maintaining them in the long term, and

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:07.570
there's specific performance
requirements in the contract.

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:12.852
I highlight a couple on this slide,
for example, elevators, and

00:25:12.852 --> 00:25:17.870
elevators out for maintenance
was a big concern of city staff.

00:25:17.870 --> 00:25:20.993
And so, there's a specific
requirement in the contract for

00:25:20.993 --> 00:25:24.364
the contractor to essentially
repair elevators that are down for

00:25:24.364 --> 00:25:29.410
maintenance quickly, and if they they
don't, the city's payments are reduced.

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:34.000
In fact, if enough elevators are out,
then the city

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:38.860
can deem part of some of the upper floors
of the city hall not available, and

00:25:38.860 --> 00:25:44.460
does not pay rent effectively
on that part of the building.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:49.393
The maintenance requirements also
have a handback requirement at

00:25:49.393 --> 00:25:52.219
the end of that 35 year concession.

00:25:52.219 --> 00:25:56.561
So essentially,
the Facility Condition Index, or FCI,

00:25:56.561 --> 00:26:01.650
has to still remain 20% at
the end of that 35 year period.

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:07.440
So that essentially means that
the building will be 20% deteriorated.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:10.870
To put that into context,
the old building,

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:14.945
which was about 40 years old,
had an FCI of 52%.

00:26:14.945 --> 00:26:19.934
And the library,
the old library that's being replaced,

00:26:19.934 --> 00:26:24.341
had an FCI of 73% at the time
the project started.

00:26:24.341 --> 00:26:27.402
To put those numbers into perspective,

00:26:27.402 --> 00:26:33.120
those are clearly in the tear down
territory for a public building.

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:37.000
And so, the city,
at the end of the concession period will

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:43.840
be able to inspect the facility to make
sure that it meets that FCI of 20%.

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:49.880
The last aspect of this that I wanted to
highlight was, the city was able to tailor

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:55.950
the contract based on the different
public agencies involved in the project.

00:26:55.950 --> 00:27:00.230
So as I mentioned earlier, the city was
having a deferred maintenance problem, so

00:27:00.230 --> 00:27:05.480
they transferred more of the maintenance
responsibility to the private contractor.

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:10.730
The port facility is also going to
be maintained by the contractor, but

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:15.010
the port didn't have the same maintenance
problem on its existing assets.

00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:20.590
And it wanted to perform more of the basic
maintenance using its internal staff or

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:22.110
contractors.

00:27:22.110 --> 00:27:22.890
And so,

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:27.020
for the port building, the contractor's
really going to be more responsible for

00:27:27.020 --> 00:27:31.480
major maintenance and rehabilitation,
as opposed to minor maintenance.

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:36.460
The other, of course,
unique factor of this

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:41.380
project is because it's funded on
an annual availability payment.

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:46.453
The city effectively doesn't make
payments on the city hall or

00:27:46.453 --> 00:27:51.914
library facilities until they're
kind of turned over to the city.

00:27:54.743 --> 00:27:58.050
Well, that was my case and
topic for today.

00:27:58.050 --> 00:28:01.770
I hope it was useful and if you're
interested in learning more about these

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:05.870
concepts, our website's
www.gpc.stanford.edu.

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:09.670
And it'd be great to turn it over for
some questions, thanks.

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:14.060
&gt;&gt; Mike, as need for
water infrastructure funding increases,

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:19.260
do you think bond funding, less than
$50 million, will be in higher demand?

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:22.480
Also, do you think bond financing and
community involvement,

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:25.610
community members participating
in bond financing, will increase?

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:29.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that’s a great question.

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:34.320
I definitely think that, of course,
as there’s increasing demand for

00:28:34.320 --> 00:28:38.810
infrastructure, investment
in smaller projects.

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:40.432
Most of those projects and

00:28:40.432 --> 00:28:45.089
most infrastructure generally is
going to use traditional procurement.

00:28:45.089 --> 00:28:48.366
And, of course,
that could entail some bond financing, so

00:28:48.366 --> 00:28:50.180
I do think that it’ll increase.

00:28:51.310 --> 00:28:56.779
In terms of the other part of your
question, just focused on Project 5,

00:28:56.779 --> 00:29:00.917
so this procurement model is
used in the water sector.

00:29:00.917 --> 00:29:05.100
And there's also some other forms
of alternative procurement,

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:09.511
in which it would be used for
a long-term maintenance contract, but

00:29:09.511 --> 00:29:12.946
there wouldn't necessarily
be private financing.

00:29:12.946 --> 00:29:18.116
And so, I think those
procurement models will increase

00:29:18.116 --> 00:29:26.410
as more water utilities kind of turn to
kind of long-term maintenance contracts.

00:29:26.410 --> 00:29:27.990
In terms of project size,

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:32.440
as I mentioned, this procurement model
is generally used for smaller projects.

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:37.260
I put $100 million dollars as a good floor

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:40.290
just to illustrate
the complexity of the project.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:45.410
Where there is an opportunity for
smaller projects is in project bundling.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:50.410
So that would be a case in which multiple
utilities that require an investment

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:53.690
effectively bundle their
projects together,

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:56.730
and procure one contract for
all of the investments.

00:29:56.730 --> 00:30:00.660
So that would be the real opportunity for
project finance for

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:01.980
those smaller projects.

00:30:01.980 --> 00:30:03.260
&gt;&gt; All right, thanks, let's take another.

00:30:03.260 --> 00:30:07.260
So we have a participant asking, are
certain types of infrastructure projects

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:10.390
more successful when using the P3 models?

00:30:10.390 --> 00:30:14.919
And he's given the example of statistics
with toll roads, airports, etc.

00:30:14.919 --> 00:30:20.007
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so in general,
I would say that the projects that

00:30:20.007 --> 00:30:25.768
entail more risk are better
suited to this procurement model.

00:30:25.768 --> 00:30:30.049
So a project such as a toll
road that has funding risks,

00:30:30.049 --> 00:30:35.929
if there's not enough drivers for
the road that entails additional risk.

00:30:35.929 --> 00:30:40.798
And therefore, this procurement model
is a strong fit for that sector.

00:30:40.798 --> 00:30:46.062
Airports is interesting,
I mentioned the US is an outlier for

00:30:46.062 --> 00:30:50.213
this procurement model,
and airports is the one

00:30:50.213 --> 00:30:54.784
sector where the US is
probably the largest outlier.

00:30:54.784 --> 00:30:59.612
So most airports that are in
other international developed

00:30:59.612 --> 00:31:04.820
economies have been financed
using project finance.

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:09.853
And in those cases,
the remuneration for the investors and

00:31:09.853 --> 00:31:16.811
the airport are essentially, of course,
the airline fees that use the airport.

00:31:16.811 --> 00:31:24.140
But then also, things like parking or
rents from the vendors in the airport,

00:31:24.140 --> 00:31:29.329
so there isn't necessarily
any single sector in which

00:31:29.329 --> 00:31:34.550
this procurement model is
a particularly unique fit.

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:40.219
But in the US, at least,
it's mostly been used for

00:31:40.219 --> 00:31:44.207
toll roads,
as I think the asker mentioned.

00:31:44.207 --> 00:31:48.355
&gt;&gt; Right, so did these Canada or Australia
P3 agencies have any special process for

00:31:48.355 --> 00:31:50.420
expedited environmental approvals?

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:52.370
You spoke about them in your presentation.

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:57.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I don't think they
necessarily have a process for

00:31:57.670 --> 00:32:01.450
going around traditional
environmental approvals.

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:06.520
They do have kind of the expertise to
manage the local environmental processes.

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:14.410
In procurement, so, essentially, to make
those assessments during procurement.

00:32:14.410 --> 00:32:18.550
I also think they do some
programmatic level work.

00:32:18.550 --> 00:32:22.950
So, just for example,
infrastructure in New South Wales

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:30.820
developed and maintains a 25 year
infrastructure plan for the state.

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:35.310
So that kind of long term perspective,
I think, can assist with, kind of,

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:36.690
environmental approvals and

00:32:36.690 --> 00:32:42.068
planning by simply laying out
the actual need specifically.

00:32:42.068 --> 00:32:44.970
&gt;&gt; Great, so
like staying in the global prospective can

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:49.200
developing countries an example,
like Indonesia immolate P3 approach with

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:51.505
the large projects that
you're representing here?

00:32:51.505 --> 00:32:56.005
&gt;&gt; Yeah and then developing economies
actually use this procurement model

00:32:57.355 --> 00:33:06.065
fairly often so that trend is more a
phenomenon of multilateral bank policies,

00:33:06.065 --> 00:33:13.020
so several multilateral
lenders like the World Bank

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:18.540
require that project finance be used for
some of their infrastructure projects.

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:24.010
So developing economies can certainly use
this procurement model, it's been used in

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:29.240
Indonesia even for some small or
infrastructure project.

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:32.880
They're in for, more recently,
some very large project.

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:41.220
I guess the requirement really is
that in using project finance because

00:33:41.220 --> 00:33:46.130
the investment is really based
on the project's economics and

00:33:46.130 --> 00:33:52.490
not the balance sheet or
credit of the sponsoring government.

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:55.440
For this particular
model to be used is just

00:33:55.440 --> 00:33:58.720
additional scrutiny on the project itself.

00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:03.737
So in order for this to be used in
a developing economy, the government

00:34:03.737 --> 00:34:09.275
there would simply have to adequately
appropriate funding for the project.

00:34:09.275 --> 00:34:14.134
So in that way, project finance
can be almost a tool to create

00:34:14.134 --> 00:34:17.757
additional scrutiny on the project itself,

00:34:17.757 --> 00:34:22.738
to make sure that the project
economics and risks make sense.

00:34:22.738 --> 00:34:26.660
&gt;&gt; Great, this one specifically is looking
at your case study that you presented.

00:34:26.660 --> 00:34:30.160
Who were the equity and debt investors
in the Long Beach example, and

00:34:30.160 --> 00:34:33.280
what guarantees did they have
against project cancellation?

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:36.050
Or else, another way,
who bore the development risk?

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:39.570
Yeah that's a really good question and
if you

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:45.930
may have noticed on my slide
showing the completed and cancelled

00:34:45.930 --> 00:34:51.480
procurement there's a lot of development
risk, particularly in the United States.

00:34:51.480 --> 00:34:56.710
To answer your question more specifically,
I believe the contractor for

00:34:56.710 --> 00:35:01.580
Long Beach is an infrastructure
developer called Plannery

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:04.880
and so they would be the equity.

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:13.610
I believe they actually arranged private
financing, so a private loan for

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:18.620
financing the project,
that was just based on their assessment

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:23.100
of the best way to finance the project for
them.

00:35:24.110 --> 00:35:29.290
In terms of development risk,
it is a considerable factor and

00:35:29.290 --> 00:35:34.120
the companies that procure that
compete for these projects do

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:38.780
bear some development risk, mainly
because as you can imagine, the costs for

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:44.335
them to compete for the project and
develop proposals is quite high.

00:35:44.335 --> 00:35:51.005
Not sure of the specifics for Long Beach
but I believe they included a stipend for

00:35:51.005 --> 00:35:56.540
essentially if they cancel the project,
then the companies that were competing for

00:35:56.540 --> 00:36:02.070
it get a small payment
to recoup their cost for

00:36:02.070 --> 00:36:08.420
pursuing the project, and that's fairly
common for one of these procurements and

00:36:08.420 --> 00:36:15.080
it's a good practice for governments
to incentivize competition I think.

00:36:15.080 --> 00:36:18.935
&gt;&gt; Let's see, so P3's look very attractive
for long term projects until the many

00:36:18.935 --> 00:36:22.733
states face a challenge of blended state
and local funding requirements as well

00:36:22.733 --> 00:36:25.359
as moving much of maintainance
to operating budgets.

00:36:25.359 --> 00:36:29.150
How do you suggest P2 success for
a blended funding model?

00:36:29.150 --> 00:36:36.260
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question and
I think it certainly is a big factor.

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:42.210
You know, a great example of state and
local government I think

00:36:42.210 --> 00:36:47.293
that are really tackling that challenge
would be Fargo, North Dakota, currently.

00:36:47.293 --> 00:36:53.997
So they're currently procuring
a flood protection P3 and project and

00:36:53.997 --> 00:36:59.025
they're really going through
the process between,

00:36:59.025 --> 00:37:04.740
there's two states involved,
multiple municipalities.

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:10.480
And they're really going through
the process to determine who pays for

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:13.090
what and blending that funding.

00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:17.630
So the answer in terms of multiply
funding sources, or funding coming

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:22.380
from different pots is essentially
through the project development process.

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:26.090
Those desperate agencies
have to come together.

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:32.190
Another good example might
Partnerships Victoria,

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:34.490
one of the agencies in Australia,
I mentioned.

00:37:34.490 --> 00:37:36.780
So they have a practice that for

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:42.320
one of these complex procurements
they form a project board,

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:47.690
and partnership Victoria sits on that
board, but so does other agencies and

00:37:47.690 --> 00:37:52.290
sources of funding that are involved
in the project in any way.

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:56.710
The board is how those
agencies communicate and

00:37:56.710 --> 00:37:59.749
develop all the agreements necessary
to complete the procurement.

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:02.510
Thanks so much for
all these great questions.

00:38:02.510 --> 00:38:03.770
Here's another one.

00:38:03.770 --> 00:38:05.910
Considering this model is fairly new,

00:38:05.910 --> 00:38:08.490
I'm wondering if there
any statistics on average

00:38:08.490 --> 00:38:11.860
financial performance of the project
from each stakeholder's perspective.

00:38:12.928 --> 00:38:21.510
There are some aggregates,
statistics, that are published.

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:24.860
They're in some ways,
though there's not mostly.

00:38:24.860 --> 00:38:30.510
Of course these are private investments so
there might not necessarily be returns.

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:35.110
That are reported by
some of the companies or

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:39.370
funds that invest in these projects.

00:38:39.370 --> 00:38:43.990
So some of that information,
privately held by companies of course, but

00:38:43.990 --> 00:38:49.490
there are some industry publications
that publish data on, for example,

00:38:49.490 --> 00:38:55.200
the funds that have been raised
to invest in infrastructure and

00:38:55.200 --> 00:39:01.130
for some specific projects there is also

00:39:01.130 --> 00:39:06.710
the public agency that procures
them might publish funding support.

00:39:06.710 --> 00:39:10.810
So, for data like revenue
goes to investment returns.

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:15.610
&gt;&gt; It sounds like a lot of effort
needs to be invested up front

00:39:15.610 --> 00:39:19.720
to create the detailed contract
considering they are federal stakeholders.

00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:23.140
What about the overhead related
to enforcing the contract?

00:39:23.140 --> 00:39:28.100
&gt;&gt; That's an excellent point so
using project

00:39:28.100 --> 00:39:32.510
finance has higher transaction costs for

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:36.700
completing the procurement in both of
those costs that you mentioned in terms

00:39:36.700 --> 00:39:41.110
of sorting out the project lifecycle plan,
essentially.

00:39:41.110 --> 00:39:46.200
So there are additional transaction costs.

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:50.120
So when a government considers this
procurement model, essentially if you

00:39:50.120 --> 00:39:55.070
break down the assessment
into its simplest form,

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:59.220
what they're doing is they're
essentially valuing the risk that

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:04.320
they're transferring to the private
partner And they're weighing that against

00:40:04.320 --> 00:40:09.060
things like the added transaction
costs to complete the procurement.

00:40:09.060 --> 00:40:13.020
Or if the project took the higher cost
of the capital using project finance,

00:40:13.020 --> 00:40:16.980
which it should, they'll be weighing that.

00:40:16.980 --> 00:40:21.290
So they're weighing some of their costs
against the risk that they're transferring

00:40:22.840 --> 00:40:24.240
through the procurement.

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:29.510
The overhead to enforcing,
that's an additional cost as well.

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:34.460
In general,
most of these projects are procured using

00:40:34.460 --> 00:40:37.375
a performance based contract.

00:40:37.375 --> 00:40:41.102
But there's, so
most of the contracts that use this model

00:40:41.102 --> 00:40:45.149
essentially permit the government
to conduct an inspection.

00:40:45.149 --> 00:40:47.863
To make sure that maintenance
is being spent, or

00:40:47.863 --> 00:40:52.760
require the contractor to regularly
report their maintenance activities.

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:54.380
So there are some costs there.

00:40:54.380 --> 00:40:59.950
I think for a lot of these projects,
that cost in particular is relatively low,

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:02.120
but it really depends
on the project itself.

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:03.860
&gt;&gt; Okay great.

00:41:03.860 --> 00:41:05.820
Even broader question here
from this participant.

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:08.373
What will be the optimal
GDP percentage of company,

00:41:08.373 --> 00:41:11.049
I mean a country should
assign to its infrastructure?

00:41:11.049 --> 00:41:12.077
&gt;&gt; Yeah [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:12.077 --> 00:41:16.070
&gt;&gt; Take on that [LAUGH].

00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:21.210
&gt;&gt; If you read academic literature
on infrastructure, there's a lot

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:27.440
of economists that have spilt a lot
of ink on that topic in particular.

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:31.920
It does vary widely.

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:37.549
There are some interesting studies
actually coming out of China now.

00:41:37.549 --> 00:41:42.733
Mainly because China invests a much
higher percentage of its GDP

00:41:42.733 --> 00:41:48.400
in local infrastructure than
really any developed economy.

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:52.691
And part of that is because
China's of course been rapidly

00:41:52.691 --> 00:41:55.589
urbanizing over the last few decades.

00:41:55.589 --> 00:42:00.067
But there you see as a percent of GDP,

00:42:00.067 --> 00:42:04.692
I think it's getting up toward 8%,

00:42:04.692 --> 00:42:10.919
whereas the US has been between two and
3% of GDP.

00:42:10.919 --> 00:42:16.410
Personally, I don't think there's
necessarily a right answer.

00:42:16.410 --> 00:42:22.840
I think a lot of the discourse
in academia or in public policy

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:28.370
has been about targeting that appropriate
percent of GDP invested in infrastructure.

00:42:28.370 --> 00:42:31.700
When really infrastructure projects

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:37.150
really justify themselves based on their
microeconomics, not macroeconomics.

00:42:37.150 --> 00:42:42.530
So, the specific impacts of
a particular project really need to be

00:42:42.530 --> 00:42:46.770
assessed in order to be determined if it
is going to be economically beneficial.

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:50.550
But that at least might give
you a little bit of a range

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:55.078
in terms of the US which is kind of
on the low end between two and 3%.

00:42:55.078 --> 00:43:02.040
Towards an outlier on the other
end of upwards of 7% GDP.

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:05.640
&gt;&gt; Okay, we have a participant
asking you to speak about P3 for

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:08.050
aging energy assets,
such as central plants.

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:13.460
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this is, so
project finance, I didn't,

00:43:13.460 --> 00:43:19.400
my discussion today was really
focused on public infrastructure.

00:43:19.400 --> 00:43:22.600
So transportation and
social infrastructure.

00:43:22.600 --> 00:43:27.307
But for energy project finance
is actually been used in

00:43:27.307 --> 00:43:31.823
the United States in Globally
at a much higher rate.

00:43:31.823 --> 00:43:38.398
So for a P3 for an aging energy asset,
I think that the problem.

00:43:38.398 --> 00:43:42.575
Or what would need to be
addressed in procurement is really

00:43:42.575 --> 00:43:45.580
the source of funding for the project.

00:43:45.580 --> 00:43:50.010
So if it's an aging infrastructure
asset that requires rehabilitation.

00:43:50.010 --> 00:43:54.980
The government or sponsoring body for

00:43:54.980 --> 00:44:00.050
that procurement would really have
to identify a funding source.

00:44:00.050 --> 00:44:04.220
So that might be if it's
an electric utility it might

00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:08.710
be part of its rate base or some other
source of funding for the asset.

00:44:08.710 --> 00:44:10.860
So it certainly has potential but

00:44:10.860 --> 00:44:15.631
really it comes down to identifying
that revenue stream for the project.

00:44:17.136 --> 00:44:20.491
&gt;&gt; Do all public private partnerships
include allowing the private

00:44:20.491 --> 00:44:23.360
entity to have infrastructure
that they can monetize?

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:27.522
If there are other options,
can you elaborate?

00:44:27.522 --> 00:44:28.674
That's a lot of talking [LAUGH].

00:44:28.674 --> 00:44:33.285
And we get a lot of questions so [LAUGH].

00:44:33.285 --> 00:44:41.318
&gt;&gt; So in general the using project finance
all of the cost to develop an asset and

00:44:41.318 --> 00:44:46.478
maintain it for
the long term are accounted for.

00:44:46.478 --> 00:44:50.092
So I guess to answer your question,

00:44:50.092 --> 00:44:56.722
using this procurement model
the project has to be fully funded.

00:44:56.722 --> 00:45:00.963
And the private company
that wins the contract.

00:45:00.963 --> 00:45:06.521
And also importantly the financer, the
source of debt financing for the project

00:45:06.521 --> 00:45:11.765
is going to really scrutinize this
project to make sure it's fully funded.

00:45:13.235 --> 00:45:18.485
So by that metric there
has to be a source of

00:45:18.485 --> 00:45:21.660
actual funding for
a project that uses this model.

00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:27.115
Maybe I could clarify just that

00:45:27.115 --> 00:45:32.218
using this procurement
model in almost every case.

00:45:32.218 --> 00:45:35.040
The private partner doesn't

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:38.710
get ownership of the infrastructure
asset to monetize it.

00:45:38.710 --> 00:45:42.220
So, usually it'll just
be a long-term contract.

00:45:42.220 --> 00:45:47.170
In some cases, there's privatization so

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:54.330
the asset will be transferred
in ownership to the contractor.

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:57.781
But most cases it's not.

00:45:57.781 --> 00:46:02.712
But I guess to answer your question
yes there does have to be a source of

00:46:02.712 --> 00:46:06.379
funding if project finance
was going to be used yeah.

00:46:06.379 --> 00:46:09.884
&gt;&gt; What is the strongest case we
made the government has incentive to

00:46:09.884 --> 00:46:13.090
keep infrastructure supply
in balance to demand?

00:46:13.090 --> 00:46:14.910
How can I do so without the use of prices?

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:18.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's an interesting question.

00:46:18.990 --> 00:46:24.176
I mean, there is a whole industry in and
around estimating the economic

00:46:24.176 --> 00:46:29.430
benefits of infrastructure development.

00:46:29.430 --> 00:46:34.070
But that's obviously a part definitely.

00:46:34.070 --> 00:46:38.275
There's a lot of science there, but
there's also a little bit of art in terms

00:46:38.275 --> 00:46:42.241
of estimating the benefits to
a government of developing a new project.

00:46:42.241 --> 00:46:45.170
It's difficult to tell.

00:46:45.170 --> 00:46:52.290
You know I think for
some assets there can be pricing.

00:46:52.290 --> 00:46:58.410
So I do think that's part of
the reason why projects that are funded

00:46:58.410 --> 00:47:05.350
by a user fee as opposed to general taxes.

00:47:05.350 --> 00:47:10.090
It might be a more efficient way of
generating funding for infrastructure

00:47:10.090 --> 00:47:14.520
because the people that benefit from the
project are the people that pay for it.

00:47:14.520 --> 00:47:18.980
Now I think,
there should also be general public

00:47:20.230 --> 00:47:22.700
support or funding support for
infrastructure.

00:47:22.700 --> 00:47:28.716
So projects shouldn't necessarily need to
be just using toll roads as an example.

00:47:28.716 --> 00:47:33.173
They shouldn't necessarily only
need to be funded by tolls.

00:47:33.173 --> 00:47:38.967
Because toll roads also
improve the economy.

00:47:38.967 --> 00:47:41.619
And improve commute times.

00:47:41.619 --> 00:47:43.695
I just to use this as an example, for

00:47:43.695 --> 00:47:47.490
people that don't necessarily
drive on the toll road.

00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:53.151
And so because of that, I think there's
definitely a case for general taxes.

00:47:53.151 --> 00:47:58.084
But to get to your question
about the use of prices.

00:47:58.084 --> 00:48:03.951
That's why a lot of economists recommended
user fee funding for toll roads or

00:48:03.951 --> 00:48:09.650
other infrastructure because it does
give a direct indication of demand.

00:48:11.150 --> 00:48:16.620
Do you have any examples of grid
modernization projects that use P3?

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:20.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
this is a relatively new sector.

00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:25.920
I would say, I don't have a very
good example in the United States

00:48:25.920 --> 00:48:31.520
of a kind of grid modernization project.

00:48:31.520 --> 00:48:33.390
There are some related projects.

00:48:33.390 --> 00:48:37.861
So the state of Michigan recently procured

00:48:37.861 --> 00:48:42.828
a contract to essentially
upgrade its lighting

00:48:42.828 --> 00:48:47.306
systems on some freeways around Detroit.

00:48:47.306 --> 00:48:51.973
So that would be the source of funding for
that project is essentially

00:48:51.973 --> 00:48:57.310
the energy savings that the state's
going to have over the long term.

00:48:57.310 --> 00:48:59.170
And I think that the city of Washington,

00:48:59.170 --> 00:49:03.910
DC, is also in the process of
procuring a street lighting project.

00:49:03.910 --> 00:49:08.590
Chicago has recently procured
a street lighting P3, so

00:49:08.590 --> 00:49:12.070
I think that's kind of
a related grid upgrade or

00:49:12.070 --> 00:49:17.880
pole upgrade project with a clear
source of funding through cost savings.

00:49:17.880 --> 00:49:20.632
So that would be maybe a proxy.

00:49:20.632 --> 00:49:24.281
&gt;&gt; Okay, how about elaborating on
infrastructure financing for energy,

00:49:24.281 --> 00:49:27.290
especially distributed energy resources or
EV charging.

00:49:28.555 --> 00:49:33.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so

00:49:33.970 --> 00:49:39.390
I guess the trouble with more distributed
projects or EB charging projects for

00:49:39.390 --> 00:49:44.730
cities, there have been some smaller
contracts to do that in the US.

00:49:46.040 --> 00:49:50.940
In general I think those projects
to really use project finance or

00:49:50.940 --> 00:49:56.260
to be procured as a public-private
partnership, they usually have to be

00:49:56.260 --> 00:50:02.020
bundled because they're generally
kind of for smaller scale projects.

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:11.098
But the procurement model could
certainly be used for those.

00:50:11.098 --> 00:50:16.452
I think for new technologies,
like sometimes this

00:50:16.452 --> 00:50:21.939
procurement model,
it can be slower to be adopted.

00:50:21.939 --> 00:50:25.707
So in this case,
if there's an EV contract or

00:50:25.707 --> 00:50:31.580
kind of a I believe it was
a distributed energy kind of a project.

00:50:31.580 --> 00:50:36.290
So the most likely source of
revenue there, of course,

00:50:36.290 --> 00:50:43.920
will be energy fees from users of those EV
charging stations or from the community.

00:50:43.920 --> 00:50:47.206
And so
because it's a relatively new sector,

00:50:47.206 --> 00:50:51.936
it's kind of hard for
companies to really forecast that revenue.

00:50:51.936 --> 00:50:55.573
So in some cases,
I would imagine for an EV project,

00:50:55.573 --> 00:51:00.866
if a city wants to pursue one of those
using a public-private partnership,

00:51:00.866 --> 00:51:06.406
they might be able to transfer some of
that demand risk to a private partner, but

00:51:06.406 --> 00:51:11.812
also potentially guarantee some payment
over the long term for the project.

00:51:11.812 --> 00:51:14.678
So it's possible, but it's difficult for

00:51:14.678 --> 00:51:17.864
some new technologies to
really assess demand.

00:51:17.864 --> 00:51:19.930
&gt;&gt; Looks like we have time for
about one more question.

00:51:19.930 --> 00:51:22.820
Who is the right expert to
help develop legislation or

00:51:22.820 --> 00:51:27.070
a ballot initiative to create an optimized
version of the Canada-Australia special

00:51:27.070 --> 00:51:29.426
procurement authority to encourage P3s?

00:51:29.426 --> 00:51:31.876
Seems like a good question to end on.

00:51:31.876 --> 00:51:37.282
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so it's interesting,
I mean, there's lobbyists for

00:51:37.282 --> 00:51:44.222
the infrastructure industry just like
there are lobbyists for every industry.

00:51:44.222 --> 00:51:49.900
And there is certainly experts
that have kind of looked at

00:51:49.900 --> 00:51:55.829
the US federal policy in ways
to incentivize some of these.

00:51:55.829 --> 00:52:00.791
And so I think there's some expert groups

00:52:00.791 --> 00:52:05.341
out there on infrastructure policy.

00:52:05.341 --> 00:52:10.265
I guess one of the factors, of course,
is that the agencies I mentioned

00:52:10.265 --> 00:52:15.519
aren't necessarily federal agencies,
although there have been some good

00:52:15.519 --> 00:52:20.800
programs created like the Build America
Bureau at the federal government.

00:52:20.800 --> 00:52:23.310
These are state level agencies, so

00:52:23.310 --> 00:52:27.730
it's not necessarily DC that
requires some convincing.

00:52:27.730 --> 00:52:32.972
But I think like those state
level agencies in Canada and

00:52:32.972 --> 00:52:36.540
Australia, once they were formed and

00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:41.559
they developed a track
record of operating history,

00:52:41.559 --> 00:52:45.368
they really kind of took off from there.

00:52:45.368 --> 00:52:50.099
So I think we'll see what happens with
our state level agencies here in the US.

00:52:50.099 --> 00:52:54.150
And one of them might go out on a limb.

00:52:54.150 --> 00:52:58.341
&gt;&gt; Great, with that we want to just
thank you so much for your time today.

00:52:58.341 --> 00:53:00.135
We hope you enjoyed the hour and

00:53:00.135 --> 00:53:04.419
encourage you to check out all of
our offerings at scpd.stanford.edu.

00:53:04.419 --> 00:53:07.611
And again, don't forget you'll be
receiving a recording of this webinar to

00:53:07.611 --> 00:53:09.680
share with your friends and colleagues.

00:53:09.680 --> 00:53:12.171
Thanks so much for taking the time
to attend and have a great day.

00:53:12.171 --> 00:53:12.671
&gt;&gt; Sure.

